Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING HOUSE - CHRISTMAS EVE

Period live-action Victorian London. A brass sign reads SCROOGE AND MARLEY above a fog-choked court.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Marley was dead, to begin with.

EBENEZER SCROOGE, a gaunt man in his sixties with frosted white hair, a pointed nose, blue lips, and a black greatcoat, guards a small fire and a locked coal-box.

In a cramped glass-fronted tank beyond him, BOB CRATCHIT, gentle and threadbare beneath a long white comforter, copies letters beside a single coal.

FRED, Scrooge's ruddy, handsome nephew, bursts in from the frost.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? What reason have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
Come, then. What right have you to be dismal? What reason have you to be morose? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
What's Christmas time to you but a time for paying bills without money; a time for finding yourself a year older, but not an hour richer?

FRED
I have always thought of Christmas as a good time; a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred leaves without anger. BOB involuntarily smiles after him.

MR. HARKER, a portly gentleman with a ledger under one arm, steps into the office.

MR. HARKER
At this festive season, Mr. Scrooge, it is more than usually desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute.

SCROOGE
Are there no prisons?

MR. HARKER
Plenty of prisons. Many can't go there; and many would rather die.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Harker withdraws. The court darkens behind the window.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

INT. SCROOGE'S SITTING ROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge double-locks his bleak chambers. He sits in a dressing-gown before a low fire, a basin of gruel at his elbow.

A disused bell begins to swing. Bells throughout the house answer, then cease together.

A heavy chain drags up the stairs. JACOB MARLEY passes through the closed door: transparent, pigtail and spectacles intact, steel chain wound around him with cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, deeds, and purses.

SCROOGE
You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese, a fragment of an underdone potato. There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

JACOB MARLEY
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard.

SCROOGE
Why do spirits walk the earth, and why do they come to me?

JACOB MARLEY
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellowmen. If it goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death.

JACOB MARLEY
Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence, were all my business.

JACOB MARLEY
You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

SCROOGE
I think I'd rather not.

JACOB MARLEY
Expect the first to-morrow, when the bell tolls One. The second on the next night at the same hour. The third upon the next night when the last stroke of Twelve has ceased to vibrate.

The window rises. Marley backs into the black sky, joining a host of chained phantoms that moan above London.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

At the hollow strike of ONE, bed curtains rip apart.

The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST stands beside Scrooge: child-sized yet ancient, with a smooth young face, long white hair, a white tunic trimmed in summer flowers, holly in one hand, and a clear flame rising from its crown.

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit, sir, whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am.

SCROOGE
Who, and what are you?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past.

SCROOGE
What business brought you here?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your reclamation. Take heed!

The Spirit lays a strong hand upon Scrooge's heart and draws him through the wall.

INT. BOARDING SCHOOLROOM - DAY

A cold, bare schoolroom stands nearly empty. Snow presses at cracked windows.

A lonely BOY SCROOGE reads beside a feeble fire, unaware of the visitors.

SCROOGE
I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A solitary child, neglected by his friends, is left there still.

Scrooge watches his younger self, tears gathering in his red-rimmed eyes.

FAN, a slight little girl with bright eyes and a wool cloak, runs in and embraces the boy.

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Home, for good and all. Home, for ever and ever.

FAN
Father is so much kinder than he used to be. You're never to come back here; but first, we're to be together all the Christmas long.

The children hurry out toward a waiting coach.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
She died a woman, and had, as I think, children.

SCROOGE
One child.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your nephew.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT

The schoolroom melts into a bright warehouse. Young adult Scrooge and his fellow apprentice shutter the shop as FEZZIWIG, a round old man in a Welsh wig and capacious waistcoat, leaps down from his desk.

FEZZIWIG
No more work to-night. Christmas Eve! Christmas, Ebenezer! Let's have the shutters up!

The warehouse becomes a warm ballroom. A fiddler plays; clerks and families whirl through a country dance. Fezziwig and his smiling wife lead them.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter, to make these silly folks so full of gratitude.

SCROOGE
Small!

SCROOGE
He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome; a pleasure or a toil.

The dance fades. Scrooge's joy gives way to regret.

SCROOGE
I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

INT. BELLE'S PARLOUR - NIGHT

A young Scrooge sits beside BELLE, a fair young woman in a plain mourning dress. His eyes already hold the restless gleam of avarice.

BELLE
It matters little. To you, very little. Another idol has displaced me.

SCROOGE
What idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one.

BELLE
All your other hopes have merged into the hope of being beyond the chance of the world's sordid reproach. The master-passion, Gain, engrosses you.

SCROOGE
Have I ever sought release?

BELLE
In a changed nature; in an altered spirit; in everything that made my love of any worth or value in your sight.

BELLE
I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were.

Belle leaves him alone.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Show me no more! Conduct me home. Why do you delight to torture me?

Scrooge wrests the Spirit's extinguisher-cap down over its blazing head. Light pours beneath it in an unbroken flood.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Ruddy light fills the room. The walls bloom with holly, ivy, fruit, puddings, geese, and steaming bowls of punch.

The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT rises from a throne of food: a towering, genial giant in a green fur-trimmed robe, bare feet, dark brown curls, and a holly wreath set with icicles.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in, and know me better, man!

SCROOGE
Spirit, conduct me where you will. I went forth last night on compulsion, and I learnt a lesson which is working now.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

Scrooge grips the green robe. The feast and bedroom vanish.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - DAY

A modest four-room home glows with a hard-won Christmas warmth. MRS. CRATCHIT, brave in a twice-turned gown and bright ribbons, prepares dinner with her children.

BOB enters in his patched coat, carrying TINY TIM on his shoulder. Tiny Tim is frail, supported by an iron frame and a small crutch, but his face is alert and gentle.

BOB
As good as gold, and better. He told me, coming home, that he hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

The family gathers around an enormous goose, potatoes, apple-sauce, and a blazing pudding. Their few cups are raised beside the fire.

BOB
A merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! Say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
What then? If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge bows beneath the echo of his own words.

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - NIGHT

Fred's house is bright with firelight and a crowded supper table. FRED'S WIFE, pretty and quick-eyed, laughs among their friends.

FRED
He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too!

FRED'S WIFE
More shame for him, Fred!

FRED
Who suffers by his ill whims? Himself, always. I mean to give him the same chance every year, whether he likes it or not, for I pity him.

Fred raises a glass of mulled wine.

FRED
A merry Christmas and a happy New Year to the old man, whatever he is! Uncle Scrooge!

The room toasts Scrooge. Invisible beside the hearth, Scrooge almost answers them.

EXT. CORNHILL - NIGHT

The Ghost of Christmas Present has grown grey and worn. Beneath its robe cling two starved children: IGNORANCE, a wolfish boy, and WANT, a ragged girl.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
They are Man's. This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, and all of their degree, but most of all beware this boy.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The bell strikes TWELVE. The giant vanishes.

A tall, silent figure in a deep black hood glides through the snow. Its single outstretched hand points onward.

EXT. ROYAL EXCHANGE - DAY

The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME stands soundless beside Scrooge. Well-fed merchants gather beneath the Exchange colonnade.

MR. BENTLEY
I only know he's dead.

MR. PARSONS
What has he done with his money?

MR. BENTLEY
I haven't heard. Left it to his company, perhaps. He hasn't left it to me.

MR. PARSONS
It's likely to be a very cheap funeral. I don't know of anybody to go to it.

The merchants stroll away, indifferent. Scrooge looks to the hooded Spirit for an answer.

The Spirit's hand points onward.

INT. OLD JOE'S SHOP - NIGHT

Rusty keys, chains, rags, bones, and greasy scraps crowd a low, smoky shop. OLD JOE, grey-haired and sly, weighs stolen goods beside a charcoal stove.

MRS. HOPKINS, a hard-faced charwoman, throws a bundle onto the floor. MRS. DILBER, a laundress, watches without shame.

MRS. HOPKINS
If he wanted to keep 'em after he was dead, a wicked old screw, why wasn't he natural in his lifetime?

MRS. DILBER
It's a judgment on him.

Old Joe opens the bundle. Bed-curtains, sheets, towels, silver teaspoons, and a fine burial shirt spill out.

OLD JOE
Bed-curtains!

MRS. HOPKINS
This is the end of it, you see! He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead!

SCROOGE
Spirit, I see. The case of this unhappy man might be my own. My life tends that way, now.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - DAY

The same home is dim and unnaturally quiet. Tiny Tim's little stool is empty. His crutch rests ownerless beside the cold hearth.

MRS. CRATCHIT sews with lowered eyes. The children sit still.

BOB enters slowly in his white comforter. He tries to smile and cannot.

BOB
I wish you could have gone. It would have done you good to see how green a place it is.

BOB
I promised him that I would walk there on a Sunday. My little, little child!

Bob breaks down. He climbs to the small room above, sits beside Tiny Tim's bed, and kisses the still little face.

The Spirit draws Scrooge away from the grieving family.

EXT. CHURCHYARD - NIGHT

A neglected churchyard lies walled in by houses, thick with grass and weeds. The black Spirit points to a grave.

SCROOGE
Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only?

The hand does not move.

Scrooge approaches and reads the stone: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed?

The finger points from the grave to Scrooge, then back again.

SCROOGE
I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse.

SCROOGE
I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me!

SCROOGE
Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

The spectral hand trembles. Scrooge clutches at it as the hood collapses into darkness.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Scrooge wakes gripping his own bedpost. Sunlight pours through clear windows. Church bells erupt in glorious peals.

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! Oh, Jacob Marley! Heaven, and the Christmas Time be praised for this!

Scrooge throws open the window. TOM, a quick-eyed street boy in Sunday clothes, looks up from below.

SCROOGE
What's to-day, my fine fellow?

TOM
To-day? Why, CHRISTMAS DAY.

SCROOGE
Do you know the Poulterer's, in the next street but one? Do you know whether they've sold the prize Turkey?

TOM
What, the one as big as me?

SCROOGE
Go and buy it. Tell 'em to bring it here, and I'll give you a shilling.

Tom races off. Scrooge writes Bob Cratchit's address with shaking, joyful hands.

SCROOGE
I'll send it to Bob Cratchit's. He sha'n't know who sends it.

EXT. CORNHILL - CHRISTMAS DAY

London shines beneath a clear winter sky. Scrooge, dressed in his best, walks among merry crowds with an astonished smile.

He meets Mr. Harker, who stiffens at the sight of him.

SCROOGE
My dear sir, how do you do? I hope you succeeded yesterday. A merry Christmas to you, sir!

MR. HARKER
Mr. Scrooge?

SCROOGE
Allow me to ask your pardon. Will you have the goodness--

Scrooge whispers a sum. Harker's breath catches.

MR. HARKER
My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
If you please. Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it, I assure you.

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - NIGHT

The dinner table is laid in bright array. Fred turns as Scrooge opens the door.

SCROOGE
Fred! It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Fred crosses the room and seizes Scrooge's hand. Fred's wife embraces him. The company makes room at the table.

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING HOUSE - NEXT MORNING

Scrooge sits waiting behind his open office door. Bob hurries in, eighteen minutes late, hat and comforter already off.

SCROOGE
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time.

SCROOGE
I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer. And therefore I am about to raise your salary!

Bob stares, fearing a trick.

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good fellow, than I have given you for many a year.

SCROOGE
Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

The coal-box stands open. A fresh fire brightens Bob's tank and both men's faces.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Scrooge was better than his word. He did it all, and infinitely more; and to Tiny Tim, who did not die, he was a second father.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was always said of him, that he knew how to keep Christmas well, if any man alive possessed the knowledge.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
May that be truly said of us, and all of us!

FADE OUT.
